.TH LIGHT 8
.SH NAME
aux/light \- adjust the LCD backlight on Intel graphics
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aux/light
[
.B -r
.I reg
]
[
.RB [ + | - ] percent
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Light
reads or sets the LCD backlight brightness on machines with Intel
integrated graphics by writing the panel backlight PWM duty-cycle
register.
The kernel
.IR igfx
driver (see
.IR vga (8))
maps the graphics MMIO and publishes it as the
.B igfxmmio
physical segment;
.I light
attaches that segment and pokes the register directly, so no kernel
support beyond the existing driver is needed.
.PP
With no argument
.I light
prints the current brightness as a percentage.
A bare
.I percent
(0 to 100) sets the brightness to that level; a value prefixed with
.B +
or
.B -
adjusts it relative to the current level.
The brightness is clamped to a small minimum so the screen never goes
fully dark; use the screen blanker
.RI ( vga (8))
to turn the backlight off.
.PP
The register holds the PWM period in bits 31:16 and the duty cycle in
bits 15:0, and brightness is duty divided by period.
Which register drives the panel varies: the PCH side at
.B 0xC8254
on Ironlake through Broadwell, the CPU side at
.B 0x48254
on some Haswell eDP panels such as the Thinkpad T440p, and a legacy
location at
.B 0x61254
before Ironlake.
By default
.I light
probes these and uses the one that is actually running, that is with a
nonzero period and, because the screen is lit, a nonzero duty cycle.
The
.B -r
flag forces a particular register instead.
.SH EXAMPLES
Dim to half brightness:
.IP
.EX
aux/light 50
.EE
.LP
Brighten by ten percent:
.IP
.EX
aux/light +10
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/cmd/aux/light.c
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR vga (8)
.SH BUGS
Only Intel integrated graphics driven by the
.I igfx
kernel driver are supported; on machines running the generic VESA
framebuffer there is no register access and
.I light
does nothing useful.
The correct PWM register and its polarity are not derived from the
device or its EDID, so an unusual panel may need
.B -r
or may not respond at all.
.PP
The first Intel display controller found is used; there is no way to
select among several.
